# Owner's manual

This is for {{owner}}, the person who directs {{project.name}}. The agents read
[AGENTS.md](../AGENTS.md); this page tells you what the setup expects of you and how to
change it when it gets in your way. Nothing here is fixed: the files are yours, and the
reflection loop exists so the setup changes as you learn what helps.

This project was generated by init-math, the tool that scaffolded it; you do not need it
afterwards. Terms you will meet:

- **harness**: the agent program you run ({{#if harness.is_claude}}Claude Code{{/if}}{{#if harness.is_codex}}Codex CLI{{/if}}).
- **skill**: a named procedure the agent follows when asked ({{#if harness.is_claude}}you type
  `/reflect`, for example{{/if}}{{#if harness.is_codex}}you type `$` and its name, for
  example `$reflect`; `/skills` lists them, and asking in plain words works too. They are
  not `/` commands{{/if}}).
- **hook**: a small script the harness runs before or after an agent's action.
- **nudge**: a hook that refuses an action once with an explanation and lets an identical
  retry through.
{{#if modules.gates}}
- **reviewers**: separate agents, started without the author's context, that check a claim
  before it is recorded: the claim-auditor checks that each conclusion follows from the
  evidence cited, the red-team tries to break the claim, the proof-referee checks proofs.
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.commit_gate}}
- **commit gate**: the git commit check that refuses to record a finding until the required
  reviewers have passed it.
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.conjectures}}
- **ledger**: `wiki/conjectures/`, one file per conjecture with its status and evidence.
{{/if}}

## Before you start

- The harness CLI: {{#if harness.is_claude}}Claude Code (`claude`){{/if}}{{#if harness.is_codex}}Codex CLI (`codex`){{/if}}.
- Python 3.11 or newer (`python3 --version`); `bin/selftest` and the hooks need it.
- git.
{{#if stack.tools}}
- The research tools you named in the interview: {{#each stack.tools}}{{.}}{{#if !@last}}, {{/if}}{{/each}}.
{{/if}}

## Day one

Do these the first time, in order. The next section lists steps that can wait.

You need not work through this list by reading it: start the agent in this folder and
{{#if harness.is_claude}}type `/getting-started`{{/if}}{{#if harness.is_codex}}type `$getting-started`{{/if}}, and it
walks you through these steps one at a time, doing the parts it can.

Every step below starts from a terminal in this project's folder. Open the Terminal app
(on Windows, open Ubuntu from the Start menu), then type `cd` and the folder's path, the
one the interview printed at the end (for example `cd ~/research/my-project`). Do the same
each time you come back to the project.

- **Start a session (required).**
{{#if harness.is_claude}}
  Run `claude` in this directory. The first time, it asks whether to trust this folder: say
  yes. The project's own checks (the scripts in `bin/` that only check or record) run
  without asking. For any other command it asks first; if you cannot tell what a command
  does, say "no" and ask the agent to explain it. `CLAUDE.md` imports `AGENTS.md`, so the
  agent reads the rules on its own: every agent gets `AGENTS.md`, and the main session (the
  one you talk to) also gets `COORDINATOR.md`, its own rules, from a hook at its start.
{{/if}}
{{#if harness.is_codex}}
  Run `codex` in a terminal in this directory; it reads `AGENTS.md`. The first time, Codex
  asks whether to trust this folder: say yes, because it reads the project's
  `.codex/config.toml` only in a trusted folder. Then type `/hooks` in Codex and trust the
  project's hooks: Codex skips untrusted project hooks, including in `codex exec`.
{{#if codex.git_commits}}
  That config file also lets agents commit (see "Letting agents commit" below).
{{else}}
  Agents cannot commit without you: Codex's sandbox keeps `.git` read-only, so each
  `git commit` waits for your approval, and in `codex exec` commits fail and the agent
  should tell you what it left uncommitted.
{{/if}}
{{/if}}
- **`bin/doctor`, the check that the session loaded the project (first session).** The
  getting-started skill has the agent run it: it confirms the agent was started in this
  folder, that the project's hooks run, {{#if harness.is_codex}}that Codex trusts the folder, {{/if}}and that reviewers
  run as the project's own agents. If it reports a problem, fix that first: without these
  the agents work with none of the project's checks, and nothing else shows it. Ask an agent
  to run it again whenever something seems off.
- **`bin/selftest`, the project's self-check (already run once).** The interview ran it
  when it created the project. Run it again (in the terminal, outside the agent) after you or
  an agent change the setup itself: hooks, `bin/` scripts, `project.toml`. Every line should
  say PASS. A FAIL means part of the setup is broken: run
  `bin/selftest -v` for details and paste them to an agent to fix.
{{#if modules.questions}}
{{#if seeds.active_question}}
- **Start on the question you chose (recommended).** The question files were drafted from
  your interview answers and live at `wiki/questions/Q001-<slug>.md` (and Q002, ... if there
  are more). In the interview you chose {{seeds.active_question}} as the one question to
  start on, so it is already `active` (`activated-by: {{owner}}`) and agents can work on it
  now. Read
  it once more, then let the agents do a first, focused round of real work on it: one question
  makes it easy to see how the setup behaves before you widen it. Any other questions start
  as `proposed`. Activate one when you want it worked on: in the header between the `---`
  lines at the top of its file, set `status: active` and `activated-by: {{owner}}`, or
  ask an agent to ("Activate Q002"). `bin/question-check` then confirms it is active; a typo there
  would silently keep it out of the agents' work.
  Agents work only on conjectures tied to an active question.
{{else}}
- **Decide the first questions (required before agents can do research).**
{{#if seeds.questions}}
  The question files were drafted from your interview answers and live at
  `wiki/questions/Q001-<slug>.md` (and Q002, ... if there are more). Read them. They start as
  `proposed`. When a question says what you want answered, activate it: in the header between
  the `---` lines at the top of its file, set `status: active` and `activated-by: {{owner}}`,
  or ask an agent to ("Activate Q001"). `bin/question-check` then confirms it is active; a
  typo there would silently keep it out of the agents' work.
  Start with one: a single active question gives a focused first round of real work,
  and you can activate more later. Agents work only on conjectures tied to an active question.
{{else}}
  No question was seeded. Ask an agent to propose some (copy the template in
  `wiki/questions/`), then make active the one you want to start on (see below).
{{/if}}
  Until a question is active, no conjecture work is eligible. Meanwhile agents may propose
  new questions (as `proposed` files, for you to activate or
  not){{#if modules.literature}} and synthesize the sources in `wiki/inbox/_index.md`{{/if}}.
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.conjectures}}
  An empty conjecture ledger at the start is normal; it fills as agents work on active
  questions.
{{/if}}
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.literature}}
- **Synthesize inbox sources first (recommended).** `wiki/inbox/_index.md` lists the sources
  waiting to be read; it may be empty. Ask an agent to synthesize them before experimenting:
  the agents are told not to work straight off unread sources.
{{/if}}
- **Ask an agent what it would do next, then let it work (required, in the end).** For
  example: "What would you do next on the active question? Tell me the plan before you
  start." A good answer names a concrete first step tied to the question; if it is vague, ask
  for specifics. The setup is meant to keep the agent honest without you watching every step.

