## What only you do

These are your decisions. An agent may propose one but never decides one on its own or
because a file says to. Where an item says "or ask an agent to", the agent makes the edit
when you ask in the conversation.{{#if modules.commit_gate}}{{#if modules.questions}} The commit hook
(`.githooks/pre-commit`, which runs [`bin/question-check`](../bin/question-check)) refuses a
commit while an active question lacks its `activated-by:` line or any question or conjecture
file fails the check. It cannot tell whether you or an agent wrote that line: it is a
reminder and a record.{{/if}}{{/if}}
If an agent makes one of these changes without your say-so, undo it.

- **Set standing procedures.** In `notes/procedures.md`, write the answers you would give
  every time to an agent's clarifying questions (how large a first run should be, which
  method to try first, what to report), one line each. Before a long computation or a new
  line of work, an agent takes each answer from there and
  asks you only about the rest, in one question. Agents propose additions when you have
  answered the same question the same way twice; only you edit the file.
{{#if modules.attempts}}
- **Switch off or retire a principle.** In `docs/principles.toml`, `on = false` hides a
  principle from the usage report; it does not change the rule's text in the docs, which
  changes only as a workflow change you approve. Agents propose these at program review.
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.questions}}
- **Activate a research question.** Change `status: proposed` to `status: active` in the
  question file and add a line `activated-by: {{owner}}`, or ask an agent to.
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.conjectures}}
- **Escalate a conjecture.** Add `@priority` to the conjecture file, with an
  `activated-by: {{owner}}` line nearby, or ask an agent to.
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.conjectures}}
- **Approve promoting a conjecture** to `supported`, `scope-tight` or `proved` by our own
  proof. The agent proposes after the reviewers have passed it; you say yes in conversation.
  Nothing records your yes{{#if modules.commit_gate}} (the commit gate checks only the
  reviewers' verdicts){{/if}}, so if an agent promotes one without asking you, undo it.
  A `proved` that rests on a cited result (a `settled-by:` line) the agent may set itself
  after the claim-auditor passes it (see [conjecture-ledger.md](discipline/conjecture-ledger.md));
  it tells you in its next reply, naming the theorem and the source, so you can undo a
  misreading while it is cheap.
{{/if}}
- **Approve an ASK change from the reflection loop** (see below): a change to this setup that
  only you may approve, such as an edit to AGENTS.md{{#if harness.coordinator_file}} or {{harness.coordinator_file}}{{/if}}, a widened permission or a new
  blocking check.
- **Publish** anything outside this repository: a preprint, a public repository, a post.
{{#if modules.commit_gate}}
- **Bypass the commit gate** with `GATE_OK=1 git commit …` when a review is genuinely not
  needed. {{#if modules.guard}}The safety guard blocks agents from doing this{{else}}Agents are told never to do this (with the safety guard off, nothing blocks it){{/if}}; your bypasses are logged to
  `logs/feedback.jsonl` so the reflection loop can see when the gate is too strict.
{{/if}}

## The reflection loop

Friction is recorded as it happens: failed research commands (`logs/failures.jsonl`),
moments the agent or you noticed something going badly (`logs/friction.jsonl`,
`notes/workflow-journal.md`), and your corrections (`logs/feedback.jsonl`). The `logs/*.jsonl`
files{{#if modules.commit_gate}} and `.gates/*.jsonl`{{/if}} do not exist at first: `bin/journal` and the hooks create them
on first use.

- To record a correction, tell the agent what went wrong, or ask it to log friction.
- Every so often (or when an agent suggests it), run the reflect skill{{#if harness.is_claude}} (type `/reflect`){{/if}}{{#if harness.is_codex}} (type `$reflect`){{/if}}.
  It groups what recurred and proposes fixes. Small, reversible fixes (AUTO) are applied
  and reported; anything
  that changes AGENTS.md{{#if harness.coordinator_file}} or {{harness.coordinator_file}}{{/if}}, widens a permission or adds a blocking check (ASK) waits for your
  yes. Auto-applied fixes are tagged `[auto-applied]` in the journal so you can audit them.
- Proposals waiting for you are listed, numbered, at the end of an agent's reply; answer
  "yes to 1, no to 2" whenever it suits you. Saying "continue" leaves them waiting, and a
  later reply reminds you. `bin/journal --pending` lists them at any time.
{{#if modules.program_review}}- Every few weeks, and when you change the direction of the work, an agent offers a program
  review{{#if harness.is_claude}} (`/program-review`){{/if}}{{#if harness.is_codex}} (`$program-review`){{/if}}: whether the work as a whole is moving toward your goals in
  `notes/program-goals.md`, and whether the active questions still match. Fill in that
  file's starting goals when you have a few minutes; the first review asks about any you
  leave blank. To change how often it is offered: `[program_review] every_days` in
  `project.toml`.
{{/if}}{{#if modules.retro}}- At the end of a session where you gave a lot of feedback, ask for the retro skill{{#if harness.is_claude}} (`/retro`){{/if}}{{#if harness.is_codex}} (`$retro`){{/if}}. It asks
  what standing change would have made that feedback unnecessary.
{{/if}}

## Switching off a nudge

The hooks live in `hooks/`. Most are nudges: they refuse an action once with an explanation,
and let an identical retry through.

- To silence every nudge for one session, start the harness like this:
  `HOOKS_QUIET=1 {{#if harness.is_claude}}claude{{else}}codex{{/if}}`.
{{#if modules.guard}}- The safety guard blocks a few risky commands outright: recursive deletes, `sudo`, a
  force push to the main branch, and others listed in `hooks/guard.py`. To lift its blocks
  for a session, run `export GUARD_OFF=1` in the shell that launches the agent, then start
  the harness from that shell. Do this only after reading why the command was blocked.
{{/if}}{{#if modules.stopping}}- To switch off only the continue nudge, set `CONTINUE_NUDGE_OFF=1` the same way.
{{/if}}
- To remove one nudge for good, delete its entry from `{{harness.hook_config}}` (and the
  script from `hooks/`), then run `bin/selftest`. Note it in the journal, so the reflection
  loop knows it was removed on purpose.
- Blocking checks (for example the safety guard) explain how to proceed in their message.

{{#if harness.is_codex}}
## Letting agents commit

Codex's standard sandbox lets agents edit files here but keeps `.git` read-only, so on its
own an agent cannot run `git commit`.
{{#if codex.git_commits}}
You chose to let agents commit, so `.codex/config.toml` defines a permission profile,
`project-git`: the standard sandbox plus a writable `.git`. To go back to approving each
commit, delete the profile from that file. If commits fail with a permission error, check
whether Codex was started with `-s`/`--sandbox` (including `codex exec -s workspace-write`):
that option replaces the profile.
{{else}}
You chose to approve commits yourself. To let agents commit, put this in
`.codex/config.toml` (Codex CLI 0.161):

```toml
default_permissions = "project-git"

[permissions.project-git]
extends = ":workspace"

[permissions.project-git.filesystem.":workspace_roots"]
".git" = "write"
".codex" = "read"
".agents" = "read"
```

Keep the `.codex` and `.agents` lines: without them the profile also makes the agent and
skill definitions writable.
{{/if}}

{{/if}}
{{#if harness.is_claude}}{{#if modules.firsthand}}
## Web access

`.claude/settings.json` lets agents use web search and web fetch without asking you each
time (its `permissions` block). The literature-search agent needs them, and when it runs in
the background it cannot stop to ask, so without this its searches are refused. To approve
each web call yourself instead, delete those two entries.

{{/if}}{{/if}}
## Models

You can skip this section unless you want different models.

`project.toml` maps two roles to models: **strong** (`{{tiers.strong}}`) plans, reviews
and writes anything durable; **worker** (`{{tiers.worker}}`) carries out well-specified
units. A reviewer must never run on a weaker model than the author of what it reviews.
The main thread is the strong tier: its model is set in {{#if harness.is_claude}}`.claude/settings.json`
(`"model"`){{else}}`.codex/config.toml` (`model =`){{/if}}, so a session does not fall back to the harness's default.
The models were written into that file and each agent file at init; `[tiers]` only records them{{#if modules.usage}}
(and `bin/usage-report` reads it to label each model's tier){{/if}}. To
change one, edit those model lines: {{#if harness.is_claude}}`.claude/agents/*.md` (`model:`){{else}}`.codex/agents/*.toml` (`model =`){{/if}} for agents.
{{#if harness.is_claude}}`/model` switches the main thread for one session only.
{{/if}}

**Context compaction.** When a session's conversation reaches {{harness.auto_compact_tokens}} tokens, the
harness compacts it: it replaces the history with a summary and carries on. The agents keep
everything that matters in files, so little is lost, and a shorter context makes each turn
cheaper and keeps the main thread focused. The limit is set in {{#if harness.is_claude}}`.claude/settings.json`
(`"autoCompactWindow"`){{else}}`.codex/config.toml` (`model_auto_compact_token_limit`){{/if}}. {{harness.auto_compact_tokens}} is a starting value, not a
tuned one: a lower limit compacts more often, which costs less and keeps sessions sharper, but
a session can lose track of details it did not write down. {{#if modules.usage}}`bin/usage-report` shows the largest
context a session reached; the reflection pass looks at it and asks you before it changes
the limit. {{/if}}You can also change it yourself at any time.
{{#if modules.usage}}

## What this costs

A hook copies token counts (never text) from the harness's session record into
`logs/usage.jsonl` at the end of every turn: per model, and per main thread or subagent.
`bin/usage-report` totals them by model, role, day, week or session and shows how much of
the output the main thread wrote versus subagents{{#if harness.is_codex}}, plus the latest
plan-limit percentages Codex reports{{/if}}. It shows tokens, not money: to see an
estimate, put your own per-model rates under `[usage.prices]` in `project.toml` (empty at
first, deliberately: rates change and depend on your plan). `bin/usage-report --backfill`
imports sessions from before the hook was set up, as far back as the harness still keeps
them{{#if harness.is_claude}} (Claude Code deletes transcripts after 30 days by default){{/if}}. The log is committed;
to stop it, set `USAGE_LOG_OFF=1` or remove the module. Details:
`docs/discipline/usage.md`.
{{/if}}

## Turning a module on later

This project is generated once; init-math does not update it afterwards. To add a module
you left off (experiments, Lean, ...), ask an agent to do it. Give it init-math's source (the
plugin or its repository); each module is a folder under
`skills/init-math/scripts/templates/modules/`, and its `module.toml` lists what it adds. Ask
the agent to apply that one module to this project by hand: its `bin/` scripts and hooks,
its `docs/discipline/` page, its hook registrations in `{{harness.hook_config}}`, its tables
in `project.toml`, its section of `AGENTS.md`{{#if harness.coordinator_file}} and `{{harness.coordinator_file}}` (its AGENTS fragment, rendered for each){{/if}} and its terms in `GLOSSARY.md`. Tell it to fit
them to anything you have changed here rather than overwrite your changes, then run
`bin/selftest`. Read the diff before it is committed.

